1. Begin with one wave state, written either as \(\psi(x)\) or \(\widetilde\psi(k)\), and seek an ordered family of slices \(\psi_s\).

2. Introduce a second translation coordinate \(s\). For the free, homogeneous case, \(s\)-translations commute with \(x\)-translations. This ensures that evolution does not mix different \(k\)-modes.

3. Each \(k\)-mode must therefore retain its identity and acquire only an \(s\)-dependent phase. Continuity and the translation group law require

\[
e^{-i\Omega(k)s}.
\]

Equivalently, \(q=-\Omega(k)\). Consequently, one initial slice determines every later slice: this gives \(s\) its role as evolution.

4. One mode is

\[
u_k(x,s)=e^{i[kx-\Omega(k)s]},
\]

and the complete wave is

\[
\psi(x,s)=\int c(k)e^{i[kx-\Omega(k)s]}\,dk.
\]

The minus sign fixes orientation; reversing it reverses the direction called positive \(s\).

5. Across one straight segment,

\[
\Delta\phi
=
k\Delta x-\Omega(k)\Delta s.
\]

Approximating a path by straight segments makes their phases add, producing a total phase \(\Phi[\gamma]\).

6. Linearity and successive composition make the endpoint amplitude a sum of the complex contributions

\[
e^{i\Phi[\gamma]}
\]

from all candidate paths.

7. In the many-wavelength limit, phase varies rapidly among neighboring nonstationary paths, so their arrows cancel tip-to-tail. Near

\[
\delta\Phi[\gamma]=0,
\]

neighboring arrows remain aligned and reinforce, producing the predominant ray.

8. Visually, outside the ray limit the wavelength is too large for this cancellation to isolate one path: broad diffraction and interference remain. Inside the limit, a narrow phase-aligned neighborhood survives and appears as a sharp ray. Both pictures are coherent wave interference; “broad” versus “ray-like” is more accurate than “incoherent” versus “coherent.”

